`exact?` prints a "Try this" line containing a concrete proof term. Writing that term directly gives a proof that checks quickly and does not depend on the search succeeding again. The output of the `#check` lines shows the statements the names refer to.

Expected output:
@image_mono : ∀ {α : Type u_1} {β : Type u_2} {f : α → β} {s t : Set α}, s ⊆ t → f '' s ⊆ f '' t
@Injective.comp : ∀ {α : Sort u_1} {β : Sort u_2} {γ : Sort u_3} {g : β → γ} {f : α → β},
  Injective g → Injective f → Injective (g ∘ f)
[0, 2, 4, 6, 8]

The image is defined by an existential: some point of `s` is mapped to `y`. That is why proofs use `rintro ⟨x, hx, rfl⟩` to take membership apart. The printed line shows the even numbers below ten selected from a list.

Expected output:
8
3
@le_sup_left : ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : SemilatticeSup α] {a b : α}, a ≤ a ⊔ b
@sup_le : ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : SemilatticeSup α] {a b c : α}, a ≤ c → b ≤ c → a ⊔ b ≤ c

On natural numbers the join is the maximum, so the result is 8. `⊔` is not addition and not multiplication. The two `#eval` lines print 8 for the join and 3 for the meet.

Expected output:
1 / 10
@Metric.tendsto_atTop : ∀ {α : Type u_1} {β : Type u_2} [inst : PseudoMetricSpace α] [Nonempty β]
  [inst_2 : SemilatticeSup β] {u : β → α} {a : α}, Tendsto u atTop (𝓝 a) ↔ ∀ ε > 0, ∃ N, ∀ n ≥ N, dist (u n) a < ε
@Continuous.add : ∀ {M : Type u_1} [inst : TopologicalSpace M] [inst_1 : Add M] [ContinuousAdd M] {X : Type u_2}
  [inst_3 : TopologicalSpace X] {f g : X → M}, Continuous f → Continuous g → Continuous (f + g)

`atTop` describes large indices and `𝓝 a` describes neighbourhoods of `a`, so `Tendsto` says the terms eventually enter every neighbourhood. This is the filter form of the epsilon-delta definition. The printed line is an exact rational value used as a sanity check.

